'''Paula Alma Kelly''' (October 21, 1942 – February 8, 2020)<ref>[https://deadline.com/2020/02/paula-kelly-dead-obituary-night-court-women-of-brewster-place-actress-1202857661/ Paula Kelly Dies: ‘Night Court’ & ‘Women Of Brewster Place’ Emmy Nominee Was 77] (''Deadline. com'' February 2020)</ref><ref>[https://www.nytimes.com/2020/02/12/theater/paula-kelly-dead.html Paula Kelly, Who Danced From Stage Onto the Screen, Dies at 77] (''NY Times'' February 12, 2020)</ref> was an American actress, singer, dancer and choreographer in films, television and theatre. Kelly's career began during the mid–1960s in theatre, making her [[Broadway theatre|Broadway]] debut as Mrs. Veloz in the 1964 musical ''[[Something More!]]'', alongside [[Barbara Cook]]. Kelly's other Broadway credits include ''[[The Dozens]]'' (1969), ''[[Paul Sills' Story Theatre]]'' (1971), ''[[Ovid's Metamorphoses]]'' (1971), and ''[[Sophisticated Ladies]]'' (1981), based on the music of [[Duke Ellington]], appearing with [[Gregory Hines]] and [[Phyllis Hyman]].

==Early life and education==
Born in [[Jacksonville, Florida]], Kelly was one of three daughters born to Ruth and Lehman Kelly,<ref>{{cite web|title=Paula Kelly| website=[[IMDb]] |url=https://www.imdb.com/name/nm0446780/bio?ref_=nm_ov_bio_sm|access-date = July 7, 2022}} {{Open access}}</ref> a jazz musician.<ref name=pc>{{cite news|last1=Burrell|first1=Walter|title=Paula Kelly Is Star Bound|newspaper=The Pittsburgh Courier |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/3407990/the_pittsburgh_courier/|agency=The Pittsburgh Courier|date=April 20, 1968|page=13|via = [[Newspapers.com]]|access-date = October 13, 2015}} {{Open access}}</ref> By age six, Kelly's family had relocated to [[Harlem]] neighborhood of [[New York City]]. For high school, Kelly attended the [[High School of Music & Art]], majoring in music. Kelly continued her studies at the [[Juilliard School of Music]], where she majored in dance under [[Martha Hill]]. Graduating in 1964 with a M.S. degree, Kelly performed as a soloist with major modern dance companies such as [[Martha Graham]], [[Donald McKayle]], and [[Alvin Ailey]].

==Career==
[[File:Angie Dickenson Paula Kelly Police Woman 1976.JPG|thumb|left|[[Angie Dickinson]] and Kelly in an episode of the [[NBC]] TV series ''[[Police Woman (TV series)|Police Woman]]'', 1976.]] Kelly performed as guest artist and sometimes assistant choreographer for numerous television musical specials, including ''Sammy and Friends'' (starring [[Sammy Davis Jr.]]); co-choreographer of the BBC production of ''Peter Pan'', in which she also performed the role of Tiger-Lily; [[Quincy Jones]]' TV tribute to [[Duke Ellington]], ''We Love You Madly''; ''[[The Richard Pryor Show]]''; and [[Gene Kelly]]'s ''[[New York, New York (On the Town)|New York, New York]]'', in which the two Kellys performed a duet.

Kelly performed a dance solo at the [[41st Academy Awards]] for the nominated title song from ''[[Chitty Chitty Bang Bang]]'' (1968). She appeared on the London stage in ''[[Sweet Charity]]'' with dancer and actress [[Juliet Prowse]], for which Kelly won the London Variety Award for Best Supporting Actress. She starred in the record-breaking west coast premiere of ''[[Don't Bother Me, I Can't Cope]]'' at the [[Mark Taper Forum]], for which she was awarded the Los Angeles Drama Critics Circle Award, Variety, and the first of three NAACP Image Awards.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.centertheatregroup.org/_pdf/MTF_Awards.doc |title=Archived copy |access-date=2007-12-14 |url-status=bot: unknown |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071214043820/http://www.centertheatregroup.org/_pdf/MTF_Awards.doc |archive-date=December 14, 2007 }}</ref> Kelly posed for a pictorial in the August 1969 issue of ''Playboy'' magazine, photographed by [[Lawrence Schiller]].<ref>[https://lawrenceschiller.com/paula-kelly Paula Kelly], LawrenceSchiller.com</ref>
[[File:Sweet Charity (1969) trailer 2.jpg|thumb|190px|Paula Kelly (third from right) in ''[[Sweet Charity (film)|Sweet Charity]]'' (1969). [[Chita Rivera]] is second from right.]]
Kelly's film credits include the [[Bob Fosse]]-directed film ''[[Sweet Charity (film)|Sweet Charity]]''; ''[[Soylent Green]]''; ''[[The Spook Who Sat by the Door (film)|The Spook Who Sat by the Door]]''; ''[[The Andromeda Strain (film)|The Andromeda Strain]]''; ''[[Uptown Saturday Night]]''; ''[[Lost in the Stars (1974 film)|Lost in the Stars]]''; ''[[Jo Jo Dancer, Your Life Is Calling]]''; ''Drop Squad''; and ''[[Once Upon a Time...When We Were Colored]]''.

Kelly had a regular role as Liz Williams on the first season of the sitcom ''[[Night Court]]'', for which she received an [[Emmy Award]] nomination. Kelly also guest-starred in a variety of television movies and sitcoms, including ''[[Sanford and Son]]'', ''[[Kojak]]'', ''[[Police Woman (TV series)|Police Woman]]'', ''[[The Golden Girls]]'', ''[[Good Times]]'', ''[[Any Day Now (TV series)|Any Day Now]]'' and in the [[Oprah Winfrey]]-produced TV mini-series ''[[The Women of Brewster Place (TV miniseries)|The Women of Brewster Place]]'' (based on the 1982 [[The Women of Brewster Place (novel)|novel of the same name]] by [[Gloria Naylor]]), in which she portrayed one half of a [[lesbian]] couple (with [[Lonette McKee]]) struggling against homophobia in an inner-city ghetto. Kelly was nominated for a second Emmy for her role in ''The Women of Brewster Place''.

==Personal life and death==
On September 15,{{citation needed|date=February 2020}} 1985, Kelly married British film director [[Don Chaffey]].<ref name=":0" /> Chaffey died in November 1990.<ref>{{cite news |url= https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1990-11-19-mn-3526-story.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200213152105/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1990-11-19-mn-3526-story.html|archive-date=2020-02-13|title=Don Chaffey; Directed Films, TV, Disney Features |author=Staff writers |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=1990-11-19 |access-date=2019-11-09}}</ref>

Her partner since 2003 was George Parkington.<ref name=":0"/>

Kelly died of [[heart failure]] on February 8, 2020, at the age of 77.<ref name=":0">{{Cite news |last=Smith |first=Harrison |date=2020-02-10 |title=Paula Kelly, Emmy-nominated actress, dancer and singer, dies at 77 |language=en-US |newspaper=Washington Post |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/obituaries/paula-kelly-emmy-nominated-actress-dancer-and-singer-dies-at-77/2020/02/10/39594c84-4c19-11ea-bf44-f5043eb3918a_story.html |access-date=2022-11-09 |issn=0190-8286}}</ref>

